Chapter: CatStillsSpec
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter CatStills
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 CatStills: seven baked pose tables and the index that selects them.

 HALF A MEGABYTE OF GENERATED LITERALS, WHICH IS THE POINT. This chapter is the
 largest single file in the port -- 2,198 StillPt records in one list literal for
 the rest pose alone -- and nothing about it can be wrong arithmetically. What
 can go wrong is that an arm's front end will not swallow a list literal that
 long, or will swallow it slowly enough to matter. A generated table nothing
 cites is a table nothing compiles, and it is the thing in this port most likely
 to find a limit.

 THE VALUES ARE CAPTURED AND SAY SO. bake_stills.py generates this chapter from
 the art; there is no definition to derive a want from, and the oracle for
 whether the art is right is the blitter diff against the golden PNG. So the
 sizes below are counted from the tables and the file's job is to notice a table
 that changed size, emptied, or stopped compiling -- not to re-judge the drawing.

 THE DISPATCH IS THE ONE PIECE OF ACTUAL CODE. `cat-polys-for` is a seven-way
 chain of index tests with a fallback to the empty list, so every arm is graded:
 an off-by-one in the middle of that chain returns a real pose for the wrong
 index, which draws a cat in the wrong shape rather than failing. The fallback is
 graded at both ends -- -1 and 7 -- because a chain like this is written with `if
 i == 0` and a missing guard fails at exactly one end.

 THE POSES ARE NOT ALL DISTINCT IN SIZE. Four of the seven have 34 polygons and
 1,715 points, so no count can tell coil from collapse; the first point of each
 is what separates those four, and it is graded for exactly them.

 WHAT COSTS HERE IS NAMING A TABLE, NOT WALKING ONE, and the file is shaped
 around that measurement. A nullary binding emits as a FUNCTION -- PORTING_NOTES
 B13, the same fact that keeps `no-grad` from existing -- so every mention of
 `pose-rest-polys` REBUILDS all 2,198 points. Measured: one reference is 9,110
 steps, two are 18,168, four are 36,284, exactly linear and nothing memoised.

 So every table is reached ONCE, through `cat-polys-for`, which is the dispatch
 this file has to grade anyway. Walking the whole table costs almost nothing
 once it is built, so all seven are summed in full rather than spot-checked --
 the cheaper version of this file was also the weaker one, and both facts came
 out of the same measurement.

 We say:

Section: Counting

 Everything worth knowing about one table, computed from a SINGLE reference to
 it. Splitting these into separate lines is what made an earlier draft cost
 three times as much: each line named the table again and rebuilt it.

  pts-in : List StillPoly, Integer -> Integer
  pts-in (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0
    else let p = list-at ps i in list-length p.pts + pts-in ps (i + 1)

  grads-in : List StillPoly, Integer -> Integer
  grads-in (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0
    else let p = list-at ps i in list-length p.grad + grads-in ps (i + 1)

  facts : List StillPoly -> List Integer
  facts (ps) = [list-length ps, pts-in ps 0, grads-in ps 0]

  pose-facts : Integer -> List Integer
  pose-facts (i) = facts (cat-polys-for i)

Section: The Seven Poses, Through The Dispatch

 Polygons, points and gradients for each of the seven, then both ends of the
 fallback. Reaching them through `cat-polys-for` grades the dispatch and the
 tables in one pass: an off-by-one in that seven-way chain returns a real pose
 for the wrong index, which draws a cat in the wrong shape rather than failing,
 and the counts below are what would catch it.

 NO POSE CARRIES A GRADIENT. Every cat polygon is solid; the gradients in this
 port are the bull's. That is an assertion and not an observation -- if a
 regenerated table started carrying them, the renderer would take a path this
 chapter has never taken.

  table-got : List Integer
  table-got = (pose-facts 0) & (pose-facts 1) & (pose-facts 2) & (pose-facts 3) & (pose-facts 4) & (pose-facts 5) & (pose-facts 6) & (pose-facts 7) & (pose-facts (0 - 1))

  table-want : List Integer
  table-want =
    [ 46, 2198, 0,
      46, 2198, 0,
      47, 2197, 0,
      34, 1715, 0,
      34, 1715, 0,
      34, 1715, 0,
      34, 1715, 0,
      0, 0, 0,
      0, 0, 0 ]

Section: Telling The Four Apart

 Coil, flight, land and collapse have identical counts, so the section above
 cannot distinguish them and a dispatch that returned one of them for all four
 would pass it. The first point of each differs; these four cost one more
 reference apiece and they are the only ones that need it.

  first-x : Integer -> Real
  first-x (i) = let ps = cat-polys-for i in (list-at (list-at ps 0).pts 0).x

  spot-got : List Real
  spot-got = [first-x 3, first-x 4, first-x 5, first-x 6]

  spot-want : List Real
  spot-want = [0.5062, 0.6873, 0.6467, 0.6377]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cs-table" table-got table-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cs-spot " spot-got spot-want 0.0)
  end
